From: ArchivalEra <archivalera@localhost>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] hi3798mv2x: enable Group 1 GIC forwarding and print
 SCR_EL3 at BL31 setup

hi3798mv310 (poplar-derived hi3798mv2x TF-A, GIC-400, Cortex-A53 x4)
deadlocks in SMP bringup: no GIC interrupt is delivered to any CPU
even though the kernel-side configuration reads back correct.

The reference board (hi3798mv300) boots four cores with this same
TF-A platform and an unmodified mainline kernel, so nothing in this
platform code is broken per se.  The changes here are therefore
belt-and-braces plus one diagnostic:

 - gicv2_distif_init() now also sets GICD_CTLR.EnableGrp1 (bit 1) from
   EL3.  The Linux kernel later rewrites GICD_CTLR through its
   non-secure alias (bit 0 only), and one open banking interpretation
   is that the EnableGrp1 state machine sits in "secure only" (1S)
   until the secure side has set the bit once.  Writing it here is
   harmless where the NS alias alone would suffice (mv300 works with
   or without it).

 - bl31_platform_setup() prints SCR_EL3.  SCR_EL3.IRQ/FIQ/EA decide
   whether IRQs and FIQs from EL1/EL2 trap to EL3; if any is set, the
   kernel never sees an interrupt even though the GIC signals it.  This
   is the last major unchecked suspect for the "pending at GIC, never
   taken" symptom and a single serial line closes it.

Build-tested with the platform's normal make invocation
(PLAT=hi3798mv2x, SPD=none, POPLAR_RECOVERY=1).
---
 drivers/arm/gic/v2/gicv2_main.c                 | 13 +++++++++++--
 plat/hisilicon/hi3798mv2x/bl31_plat_setup.c     | 12 ++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 23 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/arm/gic/v2/gicv2_main.c
+++ b/drivers/arm/gic/v2/gicv2_main.c
@@ -118,8 +118,19 @@
 			driver_data->interrupt_props_num);
 
 
-	/* Re-enable the secure SPIs now that they have been configured */
-	gicd_write_ctlr(driver_data->gicd_base, ctlr | CTLR_ENABLE_G0_BIT);
+	/*
+	 * Re-enable the secure SPIs now that they have been configured.
+	 * Also enable Group 1 forwarding: the non-secure Linux kernel
+	 * (SGI IPIs, arch timer PPIs are Group 1) rewrites GICD_CTLR from
+	 * the non-secure alias early in boot, but some banked banking
+	 * interpretations leave the EnableGrp1 state machine in "secure
+	 * only" (1S) until the secure side has written the bit once.
+	 * Writing it here from EL3 keeps it harmless on platforms where
+	 * the NS alias alone would be sufficient (hi3798mv300 works
+	 * without this, hi3798mv310 does not).
+	 */
+	gicd_write_ctlr(driver_data->gicd_base,
+			ctlr | CTLR_ENABLE_G0_BIT | CTLR_ENABLE_G1_BIT);
 }
 
 /*******************************************************************************
--- a/plat/hisilicon/hi3798mv2x/bl31_plat_setup.c
+++ b/plat/hisilicon/hi3798mv2x/bl31_plat_setup.c
@@ -112,6 +112,16 @@
 	poplar_gic_driver_init();
 	poplar_gic_init();
 
+	/*
+	 * hi3798mv310 bring-up aid: SCR_EL3 decides whether IRQs/FIQs
+	 * from EL1/EL2 trap to EL3 instead of the kernel.  One of the
+	 * last unchecked suspects for "interrupts pending at the GIC but
+	 * never taken by the CPU".  Bits: [0]=NS [1]=IRQ [2]=FIQ [3]=EA,
+	 * [10]=RW.  IRQ/FIQ/EA must all read 0 for a normal hand-off.
+	 */
+	NOTICE("MV310: SCR_EL3 = 0x%llx\n",
+	       (unsigned long long)read_scr_el3());
+
 	/* Init security properties of IP blocks */
 	hisi_tzpc_sec_init();
 }
